insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 1, 'What is the essential difference between the well tie of the tiers below and the wedge model of this tier?', '["A well tie uses real logs, while a wedge model uses logs that have been smoothed, despiked and resampled onto a regular grid first.", "A well tie is an observation in which several variables move at once; a wedge is an experiment in which only thickness changes.", "A well tie works on one trace at a time whereas a wedge model works across a whole seismic volume at once.", "A wedge model replaces the Ricker wavelet of the tiers below with a minimum phase wavelet extracted from the data."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The wedge holds the wavelet, the coefficients and the grid fixed and moves thickness alone, which is what makes an observed change attributable to thickness. Both use the same Ricker family, and neither works on a volume.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 2, 'Why can the teaching well not be used to measure a tuning thickness?', '["Its logged interval from 1500 ms to 1650 ms is too short to hold a bed thick enough to reach the isolated case.", "Its coefficients vary in size and sign and its beds have neighbours, so an amplitude change is not attributable to thickness.", "Its sample rate of 2 ms is far too coarse to resolve the range of thicknesses over which the tuning effect operates.", "Its sonic and density curves contain null values, which the convolution routine zero fills before the sum is taken."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Amplitude on the well is the joint result of coefficient size, coefficient sign, spacing to neighbours and thickness, and the trace delivers them already summed. The sample rate is the same 2 ms the wedge uses.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 3, 'A bed of impedance 6,600,000 sits inside a shale of impedance 5,875,000 above and below it. What is the reflection coefficient at the base of the bed?', '["Minus 0.058116, since the same two impedances meet again in the reversed order, so the numerator changes sign while the denominator stays the same.", "Plus 0.058116, exactly the same as the coefficient at the top of the bed, because the impedance contrast across the two interfaces has the same magnitude.", "Zero, because the downgoing wave has already given up its energy at the first of the two interfaces it crosses.", "It cannot be determined without the bed thickness, since the coefficient at the base depends on how far the wave has travelled inside the bed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'For a bed encased in a single lithology the numerator changes sign while the denominator is unchanged, so the base coefficient is exactly the negative of the top one. Thickness plays no part in a reflection coefficient.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 4, 'Why is the reflection pair in the capstone wedge chosen to be equal in size as well as opposite in sign?', '["Because equal coefficients keep the composite trace symmetric about the top interface rather than about the middle of the bed.", "Because an unequal pair would take substantially longer to model across all 31 traces of the panel.", "Because with the two wavelet copies equal, whatever the composite does as the bed thins comes from their spacing.", "Because a real bed always reflects with exactly equal strength at its top and at its base whatever lies above and below."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Equality removes reflection strength as a second cause, so interference is isolated. The composite is antisymmetric about the bed midpoint rather than the top interface, and a real bed has an equal and opposite pair only when the rock above and below it is the same.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 5, 'How many traces does the capstone wedge panel contain, and why that many?', '["Sixty one, one for each sample of the Ricker wavelet at a 2 ms rate with its fixed 60 ms half length.", "Thirty one, one per thickness from 0 to 60 ms inclusive in steps of the 2 ms sample interval used throughout the model.", "Ninety one, one for each sample in a single trace running from 0 ms to 180 ms of two way time.", "Thirty, one for each 2 ms step from 2 ms up to 60 ms, since a zero thickness trace carries no information at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Thirty one thicknesses from 0 to 60 inclusive. The zero thickness trace is kept deliberately, because its exact cancellation is one of the model''s structural checks. Sixty one is the wavelet length and 91 is the trace length.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 6, 'Where does the top reflection sit on every trace of the panel, and why does its position matter?', '["At sample 30, which is 60 ms; it is fixed, so any movement of the peak is caused by interference.", "At sample 0, so that the whole of the wavelet and the whole of the bed fit below it on the trace.", "At the centre of the trace, a position that moves later as the bed is thickened toward the 60 ms end of the panel.", "Wherever the base reflection is not, so that the two coefficients never share a sample and never cancel."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The top is pinned at sample 30 of 91, one third of the way down a 180 ms trace. Holding it fixed is what makes the drift of the peak measurable rather than confounded with the model moving the reflection.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 7, 'At zero thickness the capstone wedge produces a trace that is exactly zero everywhere. What does that tell you?', '["That the wavelet has been normalised so that its centre sample takes the value 1.0 at every frequency modelled.", "That the two coefficients are equal and opposite and land on the same sample, so they cancel each other exactly.", "That the 180 ms trace length is enough for the wavelet to run out at both ends without being clipped.", "That the 2 ms sample interval divides evenly into the 60 ms maximum thickness, so every step lands on a sample."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Exact cancellation at zero thickness is a direct test of the pair being equal and opposite, and it is one of the three structural checks on the model. The other statements are true of the model but are not what the zero demonstrates.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 8, 'The wavelet in the model has a fixed half length of 60 ms at every frequency. What follows from that?', '["The wavelet is 61 samples long at any frequency, so comparisons across frequency compare shape rather than length.", "The 40 Hz wavelet is physically shorter than the 25 Hz one, so its traces contain proportionally fewer samples.", "The centre value of the wavelet changes with frequency, which is why the isolated amplitude differs between the two runs.", "The half length must be increased at low frequency or the model will clip the wavelet and distort the tuning curve."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fixed half length means a fixed sample count; the higher frequency wavelet simply carries values that have decayed to nearly nothing in its outer samples. The centre value is 1.0 at every frequency, which is why the isolated amplitude is the same 0.08 in both runs.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 9, 'Why does the model search a window around the top interface rather than reading the sample at the top interface itself?', '["Because floating point rounding in the convolution can move the largest value by one sample in an unpredictable direction.", "Because the wavelet is not exactly centred on the sample grid at any frequency that does not divide the 2 ms sample rate.", "Because on thin beds the peak drifts away from the top interface.", "Because on very thin beds the base reflection can arrive before the top reflection, which reverses the order of the two events."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The peak of a thin bed composite moves early, so a fixed sample reading would depress the curve exactly where the tuning effect is strongest. Nothing about the model is random, and the base always arrives after the top.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 10, 'What is the half width of the peak search window at 25 Hz on this model?', '["Nine samples, which is 18 ms of two way time either side of the top interface at that frequency.", "One full wavelet length, which is 120 ms either side of the top interface on a trace 180 ms long.", "Sixteen milliseconds either side, the modelled tuning thickness at that frequency.", "Two samples either side, since the peak of the composite never moves further than one sample from the interface."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The window is the wavelet''s peak to trough time in samples plus one, which is nine samples or 18 ms at 25 Hz. At 40 Hz it is six samples, or 12 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 11, 'Which of the six capstone fields is calculated rather than read off the panel?', '["The isolated reflector amplitude at 25 Hz, since the thick end of the curve has to be extrapolated out to infinite thickness.", "The peak amplitude at 40 Hz, since the panel reports only the 25 Hz amplitude and the ratio between them must then be applied.", "The tuning thickness at 40 Hz, since the panel plots the 25 Hz curve alone and the second one has to be scaled from it.", "The theoretical tuning thickness at 25 Hz, which is the square root of six over two pi f and needs only a frequency."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Five fields are readings and one is an independent calculation, which is what allows the model to be checked against something it had no part in producing. The panel reports both frequencies and reads the thick end of the curve directly.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 12, 'On the wedge panel, what happens to the tuning thickness tile when the thickness slider is moved?', '["It follows the slider, because the slider selects the thickness that the whole panel is currently reporting on.", "It moves only at the moment the slider crosses the tuning point, and is otherwise held at its previous value.", "Nothing, because the tuning thickness belongs to the whole panel while the slider selects one trace.", "It is greyed out until the slider is returned to the tuning thickness, so that no inconsistent pair of readings can be taken."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Six tiles describe the panel as a whole and six respond to the slider. Expecting the tuning thickness to follow the slider is the sign that the distinction has not landed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 13, 'A wedge is built with a top coefficient of plus 0.08 and a base coefficient of plus 0.08. What does the model report at zero thickness?', '["Zero, because a bed of no thickness cannot produce a reflection of any kind.", "0.1156, the tuning amplitude, since zero thickness is where a same signed pair reaches its tuning point.", "0.08, because only the top reflection is present and the base reflection has nothing left to reflect from.", "0.16, which is twice the isolated level and the largest amplitude that curve reaches anywhere."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Same signed coefficients on one sample add to 0.16, which is twice the isolated level and the largest value that curve reaches anywhere. A same signed pair has no tuning peak at all; 16 ms is a notch for it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 14, 'Which statement about this tier''s scope is correct?', '["It rebuilds the synthetic from the teaching well''s sonic and density logs before the wedge model is run.", "It reruns the correlation scan at each wedge thickness to align the composite against the isolated case.", "It takes the synthetic and the scan as built, and adds a controlled model of what the seismic can resolve.", "It replaces the zero phase Ricker of the tiers below with a minimum phase wavelet, since real data is not zero phase."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Associate tier owns the synthetic and the Professional tier owns the scan, and this tier does not repeat either. The wavelet family is unchanged throughout the ladder.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-wedge-experiment', 15, 'Two capstone fields are graded with a tolerance of zero. Which are they, and why is an exact grade appropriate?', '["The two amplitudes at tuning, because both are stored as 32 bit floats and are therefore exactly reproducible from run to run.", "The isolated amplitude and the theoretical thickness, because both can be derived on paper without running the model at all.", "The theoretical thickness and the 25 Hz tuning thickness, because they describe the same quantity and are required to agree.", "The two tuning thicknesses, because each answer is the identity of a sample."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A tuning thickness is a grid position, so a tolerance would let three different samples count as correct, which is equivalent to not asking the question. The amplitudes are continuous quantities and are graded to 0.002.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 1, 'At the thick end of the 25 Hz capstone wedge the model reports 0.07999999821186066. What is that number?', '["The top reflection coefficient of 0.08, held as a 32 bit float.", "The tuning amplitude divided by the brightening factor of 1.4449, which recovers the coefficient to within rounding.", "The sum of the top coefficient and the small residual contribution the base reflection still makes at 60 ms of separation.", "An extrapolation of the tail of the tuning curve out to the infinite thickness at which the two reflections stop interacting."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The trailing digits are the binary representation of 0.08, not a measurement result. At 60 ms of separation the base contributes nothing measurable, so the reading is the coefficient itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 2, 'Why is the amplitude at the thick end exactly the top reflection coefficient?', '["Because the model normalises every trace by the largest value it contains before the tuning curve is plotted.", "Because a zero phase Ricker has the value 1.0 at its centre sample, so an isolated reflection contributes its own coefficient.", "Because the base reflection has an equal and opposite coefficient which cancels its own side lobes at that separation.", "Because the search window is centred on the top interface and is too narrow at 60 ms to reach the base reflection."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Convolution places a copy of the wavelet at each reflection scaled by that coefficient, and the copy peaks at 1.0. No normalisation is applied anywhere in the model.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 3, 'At what bed thickness does the 25 Hz model first come within one part in a million of the isolated level, and how does 40 Hz compare?', '["Sixteen milliseconds at 25 Hz and ten at 40 Hz, since those are the tuning thicknesses.", "Sixty milliseconds at both, since that is the thick end of the panel and the point at which the curve is read.", "Fifty milliseconds at 25 Hz and thirty two at 40 Hz, since a higher frequency wavelet has a shorter reach.", "Thirty two milliseconds at 25 Hz and fifty at 40 Hz, because a lower frequency wavelet has less energy in its side lobes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Isolation arrives gradually rather than at a boundary, and it arrives sooner at higher frequency. The tuning thicknesses are where the curve peaks, not where it settles.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 4, 'On the 60 ms trace at 25 Hz the sample 16 ms above the top interface reads minus 0.03559476. What is it?', '["The leading edge of the base reflection, which begins to arrive before its own peak.", "A numerical artefact of the convolution running off the start of the trace.", "The side lobe of the top reflection''s own wavelet copy.", "Noise added by the model so that the quality control checks have something to detect."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A single interface produces three events on the trace, a peak with a trough either side, and the trough is 44 percent of the peak. The base reflection at 60 ms of separation is nowhere near this sample, and the model contains no noise.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 5, 'The composite of an equal and opposite pair is antisymmetric about a particular time. Which one?', '["The top interface, since that is where the first reflection sits.", "The midpoint of the bed, halfway in time between the two reflections.", "The centre of the trace, at 90 ms of two way time.", "The base interface, since the negative coefficient dominates the lower half of the event."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Subtracting two copies of a symmetric function offset by T gives a function antisymmetric about the halfway point between them, whatever the thickness is.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 6, 'Which of these follows directly from the composite being antisymmetric about the bed midpoint?', '["The trace is exactly zero at the midpoint, and the peak and the trough are equal in size.", "The peak always sits exactly on the top interface, at every thickness the model runs.", "The amplitude at the midpoint equals the sum of the two reflection coefficients.", "The apparent thickness measured from peak to trough always equals the true thickness of the bed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Antisymmetry forces the value at the centre to be its own negative, so it is zero, and it forces the extrema to be mirror images. It says nothing about where those extrema sit relative to the interfaces.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 7, 'On the 25 Hz trace at 16 ms thickness, the peak is 0.11559476 at 60 ms. What is the value at 76 ms?', '["Zero, since 76 ms is the midpoint of the bed and the trace passes through zero there.", "Plus 0.11559476, because the composite is symmetric about the bed and repeats its peak at the base.", "Minus 0.11559476, since the base sits at 76 ms and antisymmetry mirrors the peak about the midpoint.", "It cannot be determined from the peak alone without knowing the wavelet frequency."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The top is at 60 ms and the bed is 16 ms thick, so the base is at 76 ms and the midpoint is at 68 ms. The peak and trough are mirror images about that midpoint.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 8, 'An interpreter picks the zero crossing of a wedge composite rather than its peak. What have they picked?', '["The top of the bed, which is the same feature the peak marks but located more stably.", "The base of the bed, which is where the negative coefficient sits on every trace of the panel.", "A point with no fixed relationship to the bed, since the zero crossing wanders with thickness.", "The midpoint of the bed, which sits half the bed thickness below the top interface."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The zero crossing is at the bed midpoint at every thickness, which makes it the one feature whose position is known analytically. It is not at the top, and the peak is the feature that wanders.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 9, 'Between 60 ms and 16 ms of bed thickness at 25 Hz, every step that thins the bed makes the event brighter. What causes that?', '["The two reflections move closer together so less energy is lost to the rock between them.", "The wavelet is negative at every lag beyond about 9 ms, so the negative base term now adds.", "The search window becomes relatively wider as the bed thins, so it captures more of the event.", "The reflection coefficients themselves increase as the bed thins, because a thinner bed is a stronger contrast."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two negatives combine into a positive contribution at the top interface, and that contribution is largest where the side lobe is deepest, at a lag near 15.6 ms. Coefficients are properties of the interfaces and do not change with thickness.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 10, 'Below about 9 ms of thickness at 25 Hz the amplitude falls steeply as the bed thins further. Why does the curve turn over there?', '["The base reflection moves outside the search window, so it stops contributing to the reported amplitude.", "Nine milliseconds is the Ricker''s first zero crossing, so below it the base contribution subtracts.", "The bed becomes thinner than one sample, so the model can no longer place the base reflection.", "The top and base coefficients begin to cancel one another in the rock rather than on the trace."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ricker is positive inside its main lobe and negative beyond the first zero crossing at pi f T equal to the square root of one half. Below that lag the base term flips sign and the curve falls to exactly zero at zero thickness.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 11, 'Two locations in a field have identical rock properties. One has a 16 ms bed and the other a 32 ms bed, and the survey is close to 25 Hz. What does the amplitude map say?', '["Both map at the isolated level of 0.08, since the reflection coefficients are the same at both locations.", "Neither can be mapped, because two locations with the same coefficients produce identical traces.", "The thick location maps brighter, in proportion to the extra rock volume that is present there.", "The thin location maps at 0.1156 and the thick one at 0.0817, so the map ranks the thin location 41 percent higher."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The map ranks locations by how close each is to the tuning thickness rather than by rock volume, so the conclusion drawn from it is exactly backwards while every number in it is correct.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 12, 'A same signed pair of plus 0.08 at the top and plus 0.08 at the base is modelled at 25 Hz. Where is the minimum of that curve and what is its value?', '["At 16 ms, with a value of 0.04440524, which is 55 percent of the isolated level.", "At zero thickness, with a value of zero, because the two coefficients cancel.", "At 60 ms, with a value of 0.08, because the curve falls monotonically toward the isolated level.", "At 32 ms, with a value of 0.08168091, mirroring the opposite pair''s value at the same thickness."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sixteen milliseconds is where the wavelet''s side lobe is deepest, so it is where an opposite pair peaks and where a same signed pair notches. Zero thickness is where the same signed curve reaches its maximum of 0.16.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 13, 'What geological configuration produces a same signed reflection pair at the top and base of a bed?', '["Any bed whose thickness is below the tuning thickness for the survey bandwidth in use.", "Any bed encased in a single lithology, since the same two impedances meet twice.", "A bed with different rock above and below it, such as a sand between shale and a tight carbonate.", "A bed whose acoustic impedance is exactly equal to that of the rock surrounding it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An encased bed always gives an opposite pair. A same signed pair needs the rock below to differ from the rock above in the same direction as the bed itself, which is ordinary rather than exotic.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 14, 'Why does the same signed case make a well tie a prerequisite for reading an amplitude map?', '["Because only a tie can establish the dominant frequency of the wavelet at the target level.", "Because only a tie can measure the peak to trough separation of the events being mapped.", "Because a tie fixes the two way time of the horizon before the amplitude is extracted along it.", "Because only impedances from the logs say whether the pair is opposite signed or same signed."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With an opposite pair a bright patch means a bed near tuning, and with a same signed pair the same thickness is dim. Nothing in the seismic alone distinguishes the two.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-two-reflections-meeting', 15, 'A sand has an impedance of 6,600,000, the shale above it is 5,875,000 and a tight carbonate below it is 8,200,000. What is the consequence for amplitude mapping over that sand?', '["The pair is same signed, so the sand is brightest when it is absent and dimmest near 16 ms.", "The pair is opposite signed, so the sand maps brightest at about 16 ms of thickness at 25 Hz.", "The two coefficients are equal, so the tuning behaviour matches the capstone wedge exactly.", "No amplitude can be mapped, because the base coefficient is larger than the top coefficient."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The top coefficient is plus 0.058116 and the base is plus 0.108108, both positive, so mapping brightness would rank the field upside down. An unequal pair still tunes; it is the shared sign that inverts the behaviour.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 1, 'What is plotted on the horizontal axis of the tuning curve?', '["The true bed thickness that was built into each trace of the model.", "The apparent thickness measured from the peak to the trough of each composite trace.", "The two way time of the top interface, which moves as the bed thickens.", "The lag at which the wavelet is evaluated, which is the thickness divided by two."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The axis is the modelled thickness, not anything measured off the trace. Apparent thickness differs substantially from it on thin beds, which is a later result.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 2, 'What quantity does the vertical axis of the tuning curve carry?', '["The value of the trace at the top interface sample, read directly.", "The largest absolute value found inside a search window around the top interface.", "The difference between the peak and the trough of the composite trace.", "The sum of the two reflection coefficients weighted by the wavelet."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The curve is the envelope of what an interpreter would pick, which is why the model searches a window rather than reading a fixed sample. The two coincide on most of the curve and diverge on the thinnest beds.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 3, 'How does the model determine the tuning thickness from the 31 amplitudes?', '["It fits a parabola through the three largest values and reports the position of its apex.", "It interpolates the curve onto a 0.1 ms grid before locating the maximum.", "It walks the 31 values keeping the index of the largest, then multiplies by the sample rate.", "It solves the derivative of the closed form expression for the thickness at which it vanishes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no fitting and no interpolation, so the answer is always an even number of milliseconds. On a tie the comparison keeps the first maximum, since it is strictly greater than.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 4, 'Why is the tuning thickness graded with a tolerance of zero rather than plus or minus one sample?', '["Because a tolerance of one sample would let three different samples all count as a correct answer.", "Because floating point storage makes the tuning thickness exactly reproducible while amplitudes are not.", "Because the theoretical value is known to many decimals and the model is required to match it.", "Because the capstone grades every field with no tolerance unless the field is a time in milliseconds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The answer is the identity of a sample, and a sample either is the maximum or is not. Amplitudes are continuous quantities and are graded to 0.002 for exactly the opposite reason.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 5, 'The modelled tuning thickness at 25 Hz is 16 ms. What bed thickness is that at the course convention of 2000 m/s?', '["Thirty two metres, because two way time has to be doubled to recover thickness.", "Eight metres, because the wave crosses the bed twice.", "Sixteen metres, because at 2000 m/s one millisecond of two way time is one metre of bed.", "It cannot be stated in metres, because a tuning thickness is a property of the wavelet."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bed thickness is the velocity times the two way time over two, so 2000 m/s gives one metre per millisecond. The tuning thickness is a property of the wavelet, which is exactly why the velocity has to be quoted with any conversion of it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 6, 'A wedge at 25 Hz is run four times with pairs of plus and minus 0.08, plus 0.08 and minus 0.05, plus 0.08 and minus 0.02, and plus 0.05 and minus 0.08. What do the four runs report as the tuning thickness?', '["Sixteen milliseconds in every case, because the thickness that maximises the composite depends on the wavelet alone.", "Sixteen, then progressively larger values as the pair becomes more unequal.", "Sixteen for the first and last, and something different for the two unequal pairs in the middle.", "The four runs cannot be compared because their isolated levels differ."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The coefficients scale the curve and the wavelet positions it. Only the height of the peak changes across the four runs.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 7, 'Derive the tuning amplitude at 25 Hz from the closed form. The Ricker at a 16 ms lag is minus 0.4449345 and the top coefficient is 0.08.', '["0.08 times 0.4449345, which is 0.0355948.", "0.08 times 1.4449345, which is 0.1155948.", "0.08 divided by 0.4449345, which is 0.179800.", "0.16 times 0.4449345, which is 0.0711895."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The composite at the top interface is the top coefficient minus the base coefficient times the wavelet at lag T, which for an opposite pair is the coefficient times one minus the wavelet value. The value 0.0355948 is the side lobe amplitude of an isolated reflection, not its peak.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 8, 'What does the ratio of the tuning amplitude to the isolated amplitude represent, and what is it at 25 Hz?', '["The signal to noise ratio of the model, which is 1.4449 because the model contains no noise.", "The brightening factor of 1.4449, which does not depend on the size of the coefficients.", "The ratio of the two tuning thicknesses at the two capstone frequencies, which is 1.6.", "The overshoot of the grid over theory, which is 1.026 at both capstone frequencies."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Any equal and opposite pair under a 25 Hz Ricker on this grid brightens by the same 44.5 percent at its tuning thickness. The 1.6 and the 1.026 are different quantities entirely.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 9, 'A horizon is known from well control to have a reflection pair of about plus and minus 0.06, and the survey is near 25 Hz. It maps at an amplitude of 0.12. What does that mean?', '["The bed there is very close to its tuning thickness of 16 ms.", "The bed there is thicker than the tuning thickness, since it is brighter than the isolated level.", "Nothing about the coefficients is wrong, since 0.12 is well inside the range the curve produces.", "No thickness can produce that amplitude with that pair, so one of the assumptions is wrong."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ceiling is the coefficient times about 1.446, which is 0.0867 for a pair of 0.06. An amplitude above the ceiling is evidence rather than opinion, and it points at the coefficients, the frequency, or the identification of the event.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 10, 'Below which amplitude does the 25 Hz tuning curve give a single thickness rather than two?', '["Below 0.1156, the tuning amplitude, since only the peak itself is unique.", "Below 0.08, the isolated level, which the rising side crosses at about 7.5 ms and the falling side never goes below.", "Below 0.04, half the isolated level, where the two branches have separated far enough to be distinguishable.", "There is no such amplitude, because every value on the curve is produced twice."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The falling side approaches the isolated level from above and never crosses it, so any amplitude under 0.08 belongs to one thickness only. The tuning amplitude itself is also unique, but it is the top of the ambiguous band rather than the bottom.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 11, 'At 25 Hz the model gives 0.09975380 at 10 ms. What other thickness produces the same amplitude?', '["About 22.35 ms, on the falling side of the curve.", "About 29 ms, which is where the curve has settled onto the isolated level.", "Twenty two milliseconds exactly, since the pairs are always symmetric about the tuning thickness.", "None, because 10 ms lies below the isolated level and is therefore unique."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pairs are not symmetric about the tuning thickness, because the two limbs have different shapes. 0.09975 is above the isolated level of 0.08, so it is in the ambiguous band.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 12, 'How does the ambiguity behave as the mapped amplitude approaches the tuning peak?', '["It widens, because the curve is flattest near its apex and small amplitude errors translate into large thickness errors.", "It disappears, because the peak is produced by a single thickness.", "It narrows, because the two candidate thicknesses converge as the amplitude approaches the maximum.", "It is unaffected, because the two branches are parallel over most of their range."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 14 ms and 17.12 ms the two candidates differ by 22 percent, while at 8 ms and 29 ms they differ by a factor of 3.6, so the ambiguity is least damaging where the amplitude is highest. The flatness near the apex is a separate problem about sensitivity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 13, 'On the 25 Hz model, where is the peak of the composite at a bed thickness of 2 ms?', '["At the top interface, since the top reflection is the larger contributor at every thickness.", "One millisecond below the top interface, at the midpoint of the bed.", "Two milliseconds later than the top interface, at the position of the base reflection.", "Six milliseconds earlier than the top interface."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the thin bed limit the composite becomes a doublet centred on the bed midpoint with its peak about 7 ms above that midpoint at 25 Hz, which puts it 6 ms above the top interface for a 2 ms bed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 14, 'Why does a systematic early pick on thin beds matter more than a random 6 ms error would?', '["Because the error changes sign across a structure, which makes the surface noisy.", "Because 6 ms is larger than the sample interval and therefore cannot be corrected afterwards.", "Because it affects the amplitude as well as the time, so both readings are lost at once.", "Because it is correlated with thickness, so it bends a mapped surface rather than averaging."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The thinnest areas pick earliest, so a bed that thins toward the flanks lifts the flanks and grows the closure. The error does not change sign, and nothing about it is visible on the section.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-tuning-curve', 15, 'Which pick is recommended where thin beds are in play, and what does it mark?', '["The peak, which marks the top of the bed and is the strongest feature on the trace.", "The trough, which marks the base of the bed and is unaffected by the drift of the peak.", "The zero crossing, which marks the bed midpoint, a known half thickness below the top.", "The first break above the event, which is unaffected by interference from the base reflection."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The composite passes through exactly zero at the bed midpoint at every thickness, which makes the zero crossing the one feature whose position is known analytically. The trough drifts as much as the peak, mirrored about the midpoint.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 1, 'What is the modelled tuning thickness at 40 Hz, and how does it relate to the 25 Hz value?', '["Ten milliseconds, which is the 16 ms value divided by the frequency ratio of 1.6.", "Ten milliseconds, which is unrelated to the 25 Hz value because each frequency has its own curve.", "Sixteen milliseconds, because the tuning thickness is a property of the reflection pair.", "Six milliseconds, which is the 16 ms value reduced by the difference between the two frequencies."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tuning thickness is inversely proportional to frequency, so raising the frequency by a factor of 1.6 divides the thickness by 1.6. The frequency difference of 15 Hz plays no part.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 2, 'Which of these changes when the wedge is run at 40 Hz instead of 25 Hz?', '["The isolated reflector amplitude at the thick end of the panel.", "The brightening factor between the tuning peak and the isolated level.", "The bed thickness at which the two reflections stop interacting measurably.", "The amplitude reported at the tuning thickness."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every quantity that is a time moves with frequency, and every amplitude ratio does not. Isolation is reached by 32 ms at 40 Hz against 50 ms at 25 Hz.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 3, 'The 25 Hz and 40 Hz tuning amplitudes are both 0.1155947595834732. Why are they identical rather than merely close?', '["Because the model rounds every amplitude to the nearest 32 bit float, which makes small differences vanish.", "Because both runs evaluate the Ricker at the same argument, since 25 times 16 and 40 times 10 are both 400.", "Because the model reuses the 25 Hz result when it detects that the curves have the same shape.", "Because the isolated level is 0.08 in both runs and the brightening factor is a universal constant."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ricker depends on frequency and thickness only through their product, so equal products give the same arithmetic and therefore the same bits. Float rounding would still leave a difference in the last digits if the calculations differed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 4, 'In the expression for the Ricker wavelet, how do frequency and thickness enter?', '["Frequency enters through the exponential and thickness through the polynomial factor.", "Only through their product, which appears squared and nowhere else separately.", "Independently, which is why each frequency has its own tuning curve.", "Through their ratio, which sets the number of samples per wavelet cycle."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With u equal to pi times f times t, the wavelet is one minus two u squared times the exponential of minus u squared, and u contains f and t only as a product. That is why there is one curve rather than several.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 5, 'At 25 Hz the model gives 0.08168091 at 32 ms. What thickness gives the same amplitude at 40 Hz?', '["Twenty milliseconds, because 25 times 32 and 40 times 20 are both 800.", "Thirty two milliseconds, because the amplitude depends on thickness and not on frequency.", "Fifty one point two milliseconds, scaling the thickness up with the frequency ratio.", "No thickness, because the 40 Hz curve has already settled onto the isolated level by 32 ms."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Equal products of frequency and thickness give equal amplitudes. The 40 Hz curve does settle earlier, but 0.08168091 is above the isolated level and occurs at 20 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 6, 'The panel reports frequency times tuning thickness as 400 at 20, 25, 40 and 50 Hz, but 390 at 15 Hz. What does the 390 mean?', '["That the 15 Hz run has a numerical error, since the product should be constant.", "That 15 Hz is outside the range over which the tuning relationship holds.", "That the 2 ms grid at 15 Hz lands nearer to the ideal product of 389.85.", "That the tuning thickness at 15 Hz was read from the chart rather than from the tile."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ideal continuous product is 1000 times the square root of six over two pi, which is 389.8484. Landing on 390 rather than 400 is closer to the ideal, and the 15 Hz peak is correspondingly slightly higher at 0.1157008.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 7, 'At a bed thickness of 4 ms, the 25 Hz model gives 0.04683 and the 40 Hz model gives 0.07182. Why is the higher frequency brighter on a very thin bed?', '["Because a higher frequency wavelet carries more energy in its main lobe than a lower frequency one does.", "Because 4 ms is closer to the 40 Hz tuning thickness of 10 ms than to the 25 Hz tuning thickness of 16 ms.", "Because the product of frequency and thickness is 160 rather than 100, so the bed sits further up the same single curve.", "Because the reflection coefficients are effectively larger at higher frequency, since the interface is sharper relative to the wavelength."]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is one curve in the product of frequency and thickness, and a higher frequency moves a given bed further along it. Coefficients are properties of the interfaces and do not depend on the wavelet at all.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 8, 'What is the ideal tuning amplitude of an equal and opposite pair of plus and minus 0.08 under any Ricker wavelet?', '["0.1157008, which is 0.08 times one plus twice the exponential of minus three halves.", "0.1155948, the value the 25 Hz and 40 Hz runs report.", "0.16, the sum of the two coefficients in absolute value.", "It depends on the frequency, rising as the frequency rises."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The maximum of one minus the Ricker is one plus 0.4462603, and no frequency appears in it. The modelled 0.1155948 falls 0.09 percent short because the grid cannot land on the ideal product.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 9, 'A survey is reprocessed to raise its dominant frequency from 25 Hz to 40 Hz. What happens to the amplitude of a bed that was at its tuning thickness before?', '["It rises by the ratio of the frequencies, since resolution and amplitude scale together.", "It falls, because the bed is now well above the new 10 ms tuning thickness and moves toward the isolated level.", "It stays the same, because the tuning amplitude is frequency independent.", "It cannot be predicted without knowing the reflection coefficients."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tuning amplitude is frequency independent, but that is a statement about beds at tuning. A 16 ms bed is at tuning at 25 Hz and above it at 40 Hz, so it moves down the falling limb.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 10, 'A target sand is 18 m thick with an interval velocity of 3000 m/s, and the survey delivers about 22 Hz. Is the sand resolved?', '["Yes, because 18 m is more than a quarter of the seismic wavelength at that frequency.", "No, because at 22 Hz the tuning thickness is 17.7 ms while the sand is 12 ms thick.", "Yes, because the tuning thickness at 22 Hz is 12 ms and the sand is thicker than that.", "It cannot be answered without the reflection coefficients of the sand."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sand is 12 ms of two way time and the theoretical tuning thickness at 22 Hz is 389.85 over 22, which is 17.7 ms, so the sand is below tuning. Coefficients set the amplitude and play no part in the resolution question.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 11, 'How is a two way time thickness converted into a bed thickness in metres?', '["Multiply by the interval velocity, since the wave travels at that velocity through the bed.", "Multiply by the interval velocity and by two, because two way time already halves the path.", "Divide by the interval velocity and multiply by two, which recovers the one way path.", "Multiply by the interval velocity and divide by two, because the wave travels down and back."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two way time covers the round trip, so the one way thickness is the velocity times the time over two. At 2000 m/s this makes one millisecond equal one metre.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 12, 'Below tuning, the peak to trough separation of the composite trace stops responding to thickness. What value does it settle at, at 25 Hz?', '["Zero, because the two events have merged into one.", "Sixteen milliseconds, the tuning thickness.", "About 13.4 ms, which is 1.0493 divided by pi times the frequency.", "Two milliseconds, the sample interval, which is the finest separation the model can report."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The thin bed composite becomes a fixed doublet whose extrema are a set distance apart, and the model reports 12 or 14 ms on its 2 ms grid. The separation never goes to zero however thin the bed becomes.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 13, 'At 25 Hz, a bed 2 ms thick is measured from peak to trough. What does that measurement report?', '["Fourteen milliseconds, which is the wavelet rather than the bed.", "Two milliseconds, since the peak marks the top and the trough marks the base.", "Twenty six milliseconds, twice the floor, because both events have spread outward.", "Nothing, because a 2 ms bed produces no measurable event."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The measurement is at its floor and is seven times the true thickness. A 2 ms bed still produces an amplitude of 0.0243, which is 30 percent of the isolated level, so it is easily detected.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 14, 'Why do the amplitude method and the apparent thickness method pair well?', '["Both work best just above the tuning thickness, so their answers can be averaged.", "Both are insensitive to the wavelet, so they can be applied without knowing the frequency.", "Apparent thickness calibrates the amplitude method by supplying the reflection pair.", "They fail on opposite sides of tuning, so between them they cover the whole range."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Amplitude is single valued and near proportional below tuning and ambiguous above it, while apparent thickness is exact above tuning and flat below it, so together they cover the range that neither covers alone.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-frequency-and-resolution', 15, 'A section is picked at 25 Hz and the peak to trough separation reads 13 ms across a wide area. What should be reported?', '["A uniform bed about 13 ms thick, since the readings are consistent across the area.", "A bed thickening gradually, since 13 ms is just below the 16 ms tuning thickness.", "That the picking is unreliable and should be repeated on a higher quality volume.", "That the readings sit at the floor and carry no thickness information."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The floor at 25 Hz is 13.36 ms, so a wide area reading 13 ms is reporting the wavelet. The apparent uniformity is an artefact and the picking is not at fault.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 1, 'What did Kallweit and Wood establish about the tuning thickness of an equal and opposite reflection pair?', '["That it equals a quarter of the seismic wavelength at the dominant frequency.", "That it equals the peak to trough time of the wavelet, whatever the wavelet is.", "That it depends on the size of the reflection coefficients as well as on the wavelet.", "That it can only be determined by modelling, since no closed form exists for it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The composite at the top interface is largest where the wavelet is most negative, and the lag at which a wavelet is most negative is by definition its peak to trough time. The coefficients scale the answer without moving it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 2, 'Differentiating the Ricker in terms of u, which equals pi times f times t, gives extrema at u equal to zero and at two u squared equal to three. What is the second of those?', '["The trough, at u equal to root six over two, giving a time of root six over two pi f.", "The first zero crossing, where the wavelet changes sign on its way out from the centre.", "The half amplitude point, used to define the wavelet''s effective bandwidth.", "The end of the wavelet, beyond which the model treats its values as zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The derivative is minus two u times three minus two u squared times the exponential, so it vanishes at the central peak and at the trough. The first zero crossing is where the wavelet itself is zero, not where its derivative is.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 3, 'What is the value of the Ricker wavelet at its trough?', '["Minus 0.4449345, the value the model reports at a 16 ms lag at 25 Hz.", "Minus one half, by symmetry with the unit peak at the centre.", "Minus 0.4462603, which is minus twice the exponential of minus three halves.", "It depends on the frequency, becoming deeper as the frequency rises."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Substituting u squared equal to three halves gives one minus three times the exponential of minus three halves. The value is frequency independent; minus 0.4449345 is what a 2 ms grid reports because it cannot sample the true trough.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 4, 'The theoretical tuning thickness is often quoted as one over 2.6 f. How does that compare with the exact expression?', '["They are the same expression written two ways.", "The rule of thumb is 1.34 percent low, at 15.3846 ms against 15.5939 ms.", "The rule of thumb is 2.6 percent high, which is the same as the grid overshoot.", "The rule of thumb applies to a Ricker while the exact form applies to any wavelet."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Root six over two pi is 0.3898484 while one over 2.6 is 0.3846154. The general result holds for any wavelet and the Ricker evaluation is the specific case, which is the reverse of the claim that the rule of thumb is the general one.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 5, 'Would the rule of thumb value of 15.3846 ms pass the capstone field for the theoretical tuning thickness at 25 Hz?', '["Yes, since it is within one percent of the graded value.", "Yes, since the field is graded to 0.05 ms and the difference is 0.02 ms.", "No, since the field is graded to 0.05 ms and the difference is 0.21 ms.", "No, since the field requires the modelled value of 16 ms rather than any theoretical one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gap between 15.5939 and 15.3846 is 0.209 ms, which is four times the tolerance. The field does ask for the theoretical value; the modelled 16 ms is a separate field.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 6, 'The modelled tuning thickness overshoots the theoretical value by 0.406 ms at 25 Hz and 0.254 ms at 40 Hz. What do those two have in common?', '["Both are exactly one fifth of the sample interval.", "Both are the same fraction of the theoretical value, 2.60 percent.", "Both are smaller than the amplitude tolerance of 0.002.", "Nothing, since the overshoot depends on where each frequency''s grid falls."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both runs land on a product of 400 where the ideal is 389.8484, and 400 over 389.8484 is 1.0260 whatever the frequency. The overshoot does depend on the grid fit, which is why 30 Hz gives a very different fraction.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 7, 'Why can the model not report 15.593936 ms as its tuning thickness?', '["Because the model rounds all of its outputs to two decimal places before reporting them.", "Because 15.593936 ms is the theoretical value for a different wavelet family.", "Because the model only builds traces at whole numbers of samples, so none exists there.", "Because the search window is 18 ms wide and cannot resolve differences below 2 ms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The available thicknesses are 0, 2, 4 and so on, and the argmax reports the best available candidate. This is a limit on what the question can be asked at rather than an error in any process.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 8, 'The tuning thickness overshoots theory by 2.6 percent while the tuning amplitude falls short of the ideal by only 0.09 percent. Why is the amplitude so much less affected?', '["Because the amplitude is stored as a 32 bit float while the thickness is stored as an integer.", "Because the curve is at a maximum there, so its slope is zero at that point.", "Because the amplitude is graded to 0.002 while the thickness is graded exactly.", "Because the two errors are measured against different reference values."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Near the top of a smooth curve a displacement in position costs very little in height. That is also why the amplitude fields can carry a generous tolerance while the thickness fields cannot.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 9, 'At 25 Hz the model reports 16 ms, theory gives 15.593936 ms and the rule of thumb gives 15.384615 ms. Which is the answer to the capstone field labelled tuning thickness at 25 Hz?', '["15.593936 ms, because it is the most precise of the three values available.", "Any of the three, since all fall inside the tolerance of the field.", "15.384615 ms, because the capstone follows the Kallweit and Wood convention.", "16 ms, because that field is a reading off a model that can only report grid thicknesses."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The modelled and theoretical thicknesses are separately graded fields. The modelled one has no tolerance at all, so reporting the theoretical value there loses it outright.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 10, 'Expressed in wavelengths, what is the Ricker tuning thickness of a bed?', '["A quarter of a wavelength, which is the classic resolution rule.", "Half a wavelength, since the wave must travel down and back through the bed.", "An eighth of a wavelength, which is the Widess detectability limit.", "About one fifth of a wavelength, or 0.19492 of it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Root six over four pi is 0.194924, so the tuning thickness is a wavelength over 5.13. The quarter wavelength rule is optimistic by 28 percent for a Ricker wavelet.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 11, 'A 25 Hz survey over rock at 2000 m/s is assessed with the quarter wavelength rule and with the exact expression. What do the two say?', '["Twenty metres from the quarter wavelength rule and 15.6 m from the exact expression.", "Twenty metres from both, since the two are equivalent at this velocity.", "Sixteen metres from the quarter wavelength rule and 20 m from the exact expression.", "Eighty metres from the quarter wavelength rule, which is the full wavelength at that velocity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The wavelength is 80 m, so a quarter of it is 20 m, while the exact tuning thickness of 15.5939 ms is 15.6 m of bed. Eighty metres is the whole wavelength.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 12, 'How does the Widess limit of one eighth of a wavelength relate to the tuning thickness?', '["It is a limit of detectability rather than resolution.", "It is the same quantity expressed in a different convention.", "It is the thickness at which the apparent thickness measurement reaches its floor.", "It is the thickness above which amplitude stops responding to thickness at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 25 Hz an eighth of a wavelength is 10 ms, where the model still gives a healthy 0.09975 amplitude and an apparent thickness of 14 ms. Detection has no lower limit set by bandwidth; it is set by noise.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 13, 'What makes the comparison between the modelled and the theoretical tuning thickness informative?', '["That both are computed from the same wavelet definition, so they must agree.", "That the theoretical value is more accurate, so it corrects the model.", "That the two routes share nothing but the wavelet definition, so they fail for different reasons.", "That the model is validated by the comparison, so no other checks are needed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One route convolves 31 traces and takes an argmax while the other differentiates a closed form. Agreement bounds the errors the check is sensitive to and says nothing about the rest, which is why the tier holds three unrelated checks.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 14, 'Which error would the check against theory fail to detect?', '["A base coefficient entered with the wrong sign, which would move the argmax to zero thickness.", "A search window too narrow to reach the peak on the thinnest beds of the panel.", "A grid too coarse to place a sample near the theoretical tuning thickness.", "A reflection pair of plus and minus 0.20 instead of plus and minus 0.08."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The theoretical tuning thickness does not involve the coefficients at all, so it would agree just as well with a wrong pair. The thick end check catches that one instead.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-theory-and-the-grid', 15, 'A colleague builds a wedge at 45 Hz on a 4 ms grid and reports a tuning thickness of 12 ms. What does the check say?', '["It fails, since theory gives 8.66 ms and the 4 ms grid offers 8 ms as a much nearer candidate.", "It passes, since 12 ms is the nearest available grid thickness to the theoretical value.", "It passes, since the residual of 3.3 ms is smaller than one 4 ms sample interval.", "It cannot be run, because the theoretical expression assumes a 2 ms sample rate."]'::jsonb, 0, '389.85 over 45 is 8.66 ms, so the residual of 3.3 ms is 39 percent and far larger than a grid interval can explain when a better candidate exists. The likely causes are a narrow search window or a wrong frequency.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 1, 'What is the first step of the wedge workflow, and why does it come first?', '["Measure the dominant frequency at the target level, since every result scales with it.", "Build the wedge on the project''s own coefficients and sample rate rather than round numbers.", "Get the reflection pair from the logs, because everything that follows is conditional on the answer.", "Compare the modelled tuning thickness against theory, so that no interpretation rests on an unchecked model."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A same signed pair notches where an opposite pair peaks, so the sign question decides whether the rest of the tier applies at all. Frequency, building and checking follow it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 2, 'Where should the frequency used in a wedge model come from?', '["The acquisition design, which states the sweep or source spectrum that was planned.", "The processing report, which states the bandwidth delivered by the final migration.", "A value chosen so that the model reproduces the thickness measured in a nearby well.", "The data itself, measured at the depth of the target."]'::jsonb, 3, 'High frequencies are lost with depth, so the delivered bandwidth at the target is what matters. Choosing the frequency to fit a well turns a measurement into a fitting parameter and destroys the model''s independence.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 3, 'Which of these orderings makes a wedge model a rationalisation rather than a measurement?', '["Reading the amplitude map first and then building the wedge to explain what was seen.", "Building the wedge before the amplitude map has been extracted along the horizon.", "Checking the model against theory before any thickness is read from it.", "Converting the tuning thickness into metres before the target thickness is known."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The curve will always produce a thickness consistent with any amplitude inside its range, so fitting it after the fact proves nothing. The other three orderings are either correct or harmless.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 4, 'Which three structural checks pin the wedge model at unrelated points?', '["Zero at zero thickness, the thick end, and the theory check.", "The tuning thickness, the tuning amplitude and the isolated amplitude.", "The sample rate, the trace length and the wavelet half length.", "The frequency, the velocity and the reflection pair."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each of the three tests a different property, which is why a scale error passes two and fails one. Listing the tuning thickness, tuning amplitude and isolated amplitude gives three readings rather than three checks.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 5, 'A wedge reports 16 ms at 25 Hz, a tuning amplitude of 0.1156, zero at zero thickness and a thick end amplitude of 0.0400. What has gone wrong?', '["Nothing, since the thick end of a 25 Hz curve has not fully settled by 60 ms.", "The tuning thickness is wrong, since a thick end of 0.04 implies a pair of plus and minus 0.04.", "A scale error: the thick end should return the top coefficient rather than half of it.", "The zero thickness check has failed, since a scaled model cannot cancel exactly."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tuning thickness and the zero both pass because neither depends on the overall scale, which is exactly why three unrelated checks are needed. A likely cause is a wavelet peaking at 0.5 or coefficients entered as 0.04.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 6, 'What is the most useful single interpretation check a wedge provides?', '["Whether the peak to trough separation across the map is uniform.", "Whether the mapped amplitude exceeds the ceiling the coefficient sets.", "Whether the tuning thickness in metres is larger than the target thickness.", "Whether the reflection pair from the type well is opposite signed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A ceiling that is exceeded can only fail for real reasons, so it is a fact rather than an opinion. The others are all useful and all admit judgement.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 7, 'Which pair of capstone fields is most often crossed, and what check catches it?', '["The two tuning thicknesses, caught by confirming that the frequency tile reads 40 before recording 10 ms.", "The tuning amplitude and the isolated amplitude, caught by confirming that their ratio is still exactly 1.4449.", "The two tuning amplitudes, caught by confirming that they are identical.", "The theoretical thickness and the isolated amplitude, caught by their difference in units."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two amplitudes sit next to each other on the panel and are 0.1156 and 0.0800, so a ratio far from 1.4449 says one of them is in the wrong box. Crossing the two frequencies is a real risk too, but the ratio test does not catch it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 8, 'How many panel states does the capstone require, and what belongs to each?', '["Six, one per graded field, read in the order the capstone lists them.", "One, since the panel reports every field at once for both frequencies.", "Two, one per frequency, with each frequency''s fields read together.", "Three, one for each frequency plus a third for the theoretical calculation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reading each frequency''s fields together is what prevents them being crossed, and the calculation needs no panel state at all. The isolated amplitude belongs to the 25 Hz state because that is the run it is graded on.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 9, 'What happens to a brine sand''s compressional and shear velocities when its pore fluid is replaced by gas?', '["Both fall, because the rock is softened in bulk and in shear.", "The compressional velocity falls and the shear velocity rises.", "Both rise, because the rock has become lighter.", "The compressional velocity rises and the shear velocity falls."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A fluid supports no shear, so the shear modulus is unchanged while the density falls, which makes the shear velocity rise. The compressional velocity falls because the bulk modulus drops with the softer fluid.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 10, 'A sand of impedance 6,600,000 under a shale of 5,875,000 is substituted from brine to gas, dropping its impedance to 5,055,462. What happens to the top reflection?', '["It weakens from plus 0.058116 to plus 0.005516, remaining a peak.", "It flips polarity, from plus 0.058116 to minus 0.074977.", "It strengthens from plus 0.058116 to plus 0.074977, staying the same sign.", "It disappears, since the sand and shale impedances are now nearly equal."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With brine the sand is harder than the shale and reflects as a peak; with gas it is softer and reflects as a trough. The weak positive value belongs to a different shale impedance.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 11, 'The wedge is rerun with the brine pair and then with the gas pair at 25 Hz. What changes?', '["The tuning thickness moves from 16 ms to 12 ms because the gas sand is softer.", "Nothing measurable changes, since the two pairs have similar magnitudes.", "Both the tuning thickness and the brightening factor change, because both depend on the rock.", "The tuning thickness stays at 16 ms and the whole curve scales with the pair."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The amplitude ratio of 1.29013 equals the coefficient magnitude ratio to the precision the model stores. The wavelet decides the shape of the curve and the rock decides the scale.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 12, 'A gas sand at its tuning thickness reads 0.1083 while an untuned brine sand reads 0.0581. What can be said from the amplitudes alone?', '["That the brightening of 1.86 is the product of a fluid factor of 1.29 and a tuning factor of 1.445.", "That the gas sand must be at least twice as thick as the brine sand.", "That the fluid contributes nothing, since tuning explains the whole factor of 1.86.", "That the contrast is 1.86 and that the amplitudes alone cannot decompose it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The decomposition into 1.29 and 1.445 is only available because the fluid and the thickness are both known here. On a map neither is known, which is why the separation needs a thickness constraint or an angle dependent measurement.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 13, 'What does the reflection coefficient formula using impedance alone leave out?', '["Nothing, since impedance already contains both velocity and density.", "The frequency of the wavelet, which changes the coefficient at each interface.", "The shear velocity, which enters once the angle is not zero at all.", "The bed thickness, which scales the coefficient on thin beds."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The impedance form is exact at normal incidence. Shear velocity enters away from zero angle, which is what makes angle dependence carry fluid information. Reflectivity never depends on frequency or thickness.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 14, 'The brine sand has an intercept of plus 0.0579 and a gradient of minus 0.2882, while the gas sand has minus 0.0749 and minus 0.4684. How are they classified?', '["The brine sand class I and the gas sand class III.", "Both class III, since both gradients are negative.", "The brine sand class III and the gas sand class I.", "Neither can be classified without the exact Zoeppritz solution at each angle."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Class I is a hard sand with a positive intercept that dims with offset, and class III is a soft sand with a negative intercept that brightens with offset. The classification uses the intercept and gradient rather than the exact solution.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-using-the-wedge', 15, 'Why does angle dependence complicate the wedge result?', '["Because the top and base of an encased bed stop being exactly opposite once the angle is not zero.", "Because the tuning thickness moves with angle as the effective wavelet stretches.", "Because the search window has to be widened at large angles to hold the peak.", "Because reflection coefficients become complex past the critical angle and cannot be convolved."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 30 degrees the brine sand''s top reads minus 0.002583 while its base reads plus 0.009937, which are not opposite at all, so a measured gradient on a thin bed carries a tuning contribution. The full treatment of that interaction belongs to the Rock Physics ladder.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'A colleague argues that the wedge model should be replaced with a study of real beds of many thicknesses across a field. What is the strongest objection?', '["Real beds would take too long to identify and measure across a field of any size.", "Real beds vary in strength, encasement and neighbours, so thickness is not alone.", "Real beds are not sampled at 2 ms, so their traces could not be compared with the model.", "Real beds are rarely thin enough to reach the tuning thickness at seismic frequencies."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The wedge exists to isolate one variable. A field study reintroduces exactly the confounding the model was built to remove, which is why its numbers would not transfer.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Which single design choice in the wedge makes the origin of the tuning curve an exact zero rather than a measured value?', '["The equal and opposite pair, which cancels exactly at zero.", "The 2 ms sample rate, which places the first trace exactly at zero thickness.", "The zero phase wavelet, which has no energy before its centre.", "The 180 ms trace length, which lets the wavelet run out at both ends."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both ends of the curve are pinned by theory: zero at zero thickness from the cancellation, and the top coefficient at the thick end from the unit wavelet peak. That is what makes the middle hard to get wrong.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'On the capstone wedge the top reflection sits at sample 30 of a 91 sample trace. What would be lost if the model instead placed the top reflection at a position that depended on thickness?', '["The tuning amplitude, which is read at the top interface.", "The exact cancellation at zero thickness, since the two spikes would no longer coincide.", "The ability to attribute a change in peak time to interference.", "The isolated reflector amplitude, which needs a fixed reference position."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A fixed top interface is what makes the drift of the peak a measurement. The cancellation and the two amplitudes would survive a moving datum.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'A wedge is built with a wavelet whose centre sample is 0.5 instead of 1.0. Which structural check fails?', '["The zero at zero thickness, since the cancellation depends on the wavelet amplitude.", "The agreement with theory, since the tuning thickness would move.", "The thick end check, since the isolated amplitude would read 0.04 instead of 0.08.", "None of them, since amplitudes are relative quantities."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A scale error passes the zero check and the theory check, because neither depends on the overall scale, and fails only at the thick end. That is why three unrelated checks are held.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Why does an isolated reflector produce an amplitude equal to its own reflection coefficient?', '["Because convolution scales a wavelet copy by the coefficient and the Ricker peaks at 1.0.", "Because the search window is centred on the interface and excludes everything else.", "Because the model normalises each trace by its own maximum value.", "Because the reflection coefficient is defined as the amplitude an isolated interface produces."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The coefficient times a unit peak is the coefficient. The definition of a reflection coefficient is an impedance ratio, not an amplitude, which is why the agreement is a result rather than a tautology.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'At 60 ms of bed thickness the 25 Hz model reports 0.07999999821186066 and the 40 Hz model reports the same value. What does that tell you?', '["That the two frequencies happen to produce the same interference at 60 ms of separation.", "That the isolated amplitude is a limit rather than a frequency reading here.", "That the two runs share a cached result, since the thick end is not recomputed per frequency.", "That 60 ms is the tuning thickness for both frequencies at that sample rate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The thick end is where interference has died away, so the reading is the top coefficient in both cases. The frequencies differ in how quickly they get there: 50 ms at 25 Hz and 32 ms at 40 Hz.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Explain why the composite trace of an equal and opposite pair has a peak and a trough of exactly equal size at every thickness.', '["Because the search window is symmetric about the top interface.", "Because the wavelet is zero phase and therefore symmetric about its own centre.", "Because the two reflection coefficients are equal in magnitude, so their wavelet copies are too.", "Because the composite is antisymmetric about the bed midpoint."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Antisymmetry follows from subtracting two copies of a symmetric function offset by the bed thickness. Equal coefficients and a symmetric wavelet are ingredients of that result rather than the result itself.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'On a 25 Hz composite at 16 ms thickness, at which time is the trace exactly zero?', '["At 60 ms, the top interface, since the two contributions cancel there.", "At 68 ms, the midpoint of the bed.", "At 76 ms, the base interface, where the negative coefficient sits.", "Nowhere, since the trace only crosses zero between samples."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The midpoint is halfway between the top at 60 ms and the base at 76 ms, and antisymmetry forces the value there to be its own negative. It is a real zero on a real sample, not an interpolated crossing.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Which feature of a thin bed composite is most stable in position, and why does that matter for picking?', '["The peak, since it carries the largest amplitude and is easiest to track laterally.", "The zero crossing, since it sits at the bed midpoint at every thickness.", "The trough, since the base reflection is the later of the two events.", "The onset of the event, since it is unaffected by the base reflection."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The zero crossing is the one feature whose position is known analytically, half the bed thickness below the top. The peak and the trough both drift, mirrored about that midpoint.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'A bed thins from 30 ms to 16 ms at 25 Hz. What happens to its amplitude and why?', '["It falls from 0.0831 to 0.0800, approaching the isolated level as the base reflection separates.", "It rises from 0.0831 to 0.1156, since a negative base meets a negative side lobe.", "It stays near 0.08, since the reflection coefficients have not changed.", "It falls to nearly zero, because the two opposite reflections begin to cancel."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Beyond a 9 ms lag the Ricker is negative, so a negative base coefficient contributes positively, and the contribution is largest where the side lobe is deepest near 15.6 ms. Cancellation only takes over below 9 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Which of these is true of a bed with a same signed reflection pair at 25 Hz?', '["It has a tuning peak at 16 ms like any other bed, but with a smaller amplitude.", "It is brightest at zero thickness and dimmest at 16 ms, which is the reverse of the opposite signed case.", "It cannot be modelled with a wedge, since the model assumes an opposite pair.", "It behaves identically to an opposite pair, since only the magnitudes matter."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The base term enters the sum multiplied by its own sign, so the special thickness stays at 16 ms while the direction of the effect reverses. The wedge handles either pair; only the reading changes.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A sand is encased in shale above and a tight carbonate below. What must be checked before amplitude is mapped over it?', '["The dominant frequency delivered at the target level by processing.", "Whether the sand is thicker or thinner than the tuning thickness for that bandwidth.", "The signs of the two reflection coefficients, taken from the well logs.", "The interval velocity, which converts the tuning thickness into metres."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With that encasement the pair may share a sign, in which case bright means thin rock rather than thick. The other three are all necessary later and none of them settles the direction of the reading.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'What is the practical consequence of an interpreter assuming a bright amplitude always means more reservoir?', '["They will overestimate thickness everywhere by the brightening factor of 1.4449.", "On an opposite signed pair near tuning they may rank a thin location above a thicker one.", "They will consistently pick the horizon early on the thin parts of the map.", "They will exceed the amplitude ceiling and be forced to revise the coefficients."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A 16 ms bed maps 41 percent brighter than a 32 ms bed with the same rock properties. The early picking is a separate consequence, of picking peaks rather than of assuming brightness means rock.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Six capstone fields are reported as 16 ms, 0.1156, 10 ms, 0.1156, 0.1156 and 15.594 ms. Which one is wrong and how is it identified?', '["The 40 Hz tuning thickness, since 10 ms cannot give the same amplitude as 16 ms.", "The theoretical value, since it should equal the modelled 16 ms.", "The isolated amplitude, since the ratio of tuning to isolated should be 1.4449 rather than 1.0.", "None of them, since all six values are inside their tolerances."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two tuning amplitudes agreeing is correct and expected, because both products are 400. The isolated field has been read from the tuning tile, and the ratio test finds it without rereading the panel.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Which statement correctly separates what the wavelet controls from what the rock controls in a wedge?', '["The wavelet sets the tuning thickness and the rock sets the tuning amplitude.", "The rock sets the tuning thickness and the wavelet sets the amplitude at it.", "Both are set by the wavelet, since the coefficients cancel out of the ratio.", "Both are set by the rock, since the wavelet is only a display choice."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The thickness that maximises the composite depends on where the wavelet is most negative, and the coefficients scale the result without moving it. That is why unequal opposite pairs still tune at 16 ms at 25 Hz.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'A wedge is run at 25 Hz with a pair of plus 0.10 and minus 0.06. Where is the tuning peak and what is its amplitude?', '["At 16 ms, with an amplitude of 0.1445, since the brightening factor applies to the sum of the coefficients.", "At 12 ms, with an amplitude of 0.1156, since the peak moves toward the stronger reflection.", "At 16 ms, with an amplitude of 0.1267, from 0.10 plus 0.06 times 0.4449.", "At 20 ms, with an amplitude of 0.1600, the sum of the two coefficients."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The composite at the top interface is the top coefficient minus the base coefficient times the wavelet at the lag. The brightening factor of 1.4449 was derived for an equal pair and does not apply here; the ratio is 1.2670.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The brine sand gives an intercept of plus 0.0579 with a gradient of minus 0.2882, and the gas sand minus 0.0749 with minus 0.4684. What do those pairs describe?', '["A class I response that dims with offset and a class III response that brightens with offset.", "Two class III responses, since both gradients are negative and both sands are soft.", "A class III response for the brine sand and a class I response for the gas sand.", "Two responses that cannot be classified without the exact solution at each angle."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Class I is a hard sand with a positive intercept and class III is a soft sand with a negative intercept, which is the classic bright spot gas signature. Both gradients being negative is common and does not by itself set the class.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'A horizon with a known pair of plus and minus 0.05 maps at 0.09 on a 25 Hz survey. What is the most defensible statement?', '["The bed is close to its tuning thickness of 16 ms, which explains the brightening.", "The bed is thicker than tuning, since 0.09 is above the isolated level of 0.05.", "No thickness can produce 0.09 with that pair, since the ceiling is about 0.0723.", "The bed is on the rising limb, since 0.09 is less than twice the isolated level."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ceiling is the coefficient times about 1.446, so 0.05 gives 0.0723. Exceeding it points at the coefficients, the frequency or the identification of the event rather than at a thickness.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'Two candidate thicknesses of 10 ms and 22.35 ms are consistent with a 25 Hz amplitude of 0.09975. Which observation separates them most reliably?', '["The peak to trough separation, which is exact above the tuning point.", "The amplitude at a second nearby location, which will differ between the branches.", "The polarity of the event, which reverses across the tuning thickness.", "The theoretical tuning thickness, which fixes which branch a bed can be on."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A separation near 22 ms confirms the thick case while one near the 13.4 ms floor says the bed is below tuning without saying where. Polarity does not reverse across tuning, and theory says nothing about which branch a particular bed sits on.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Why is the ambiguity between the two branches least damaging near the tuning peak?', '["Because the amplitude is largest there and therefore least affected by noise.", "Because only one branch exists above the isolated level.", "Because the two candidate thicknesses converge near the maximum.", "Because the peak to trough separation is exact at the tuning thickness."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 14 ms and 17.12 ms the candidates differ by 22 percent while at 8 ms and 29 ms they differ by a factor of 3.6. Both branches exist above the isolated level, which is what creates the ambiguity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'An interpreter picks the peak of an event over a bed 4 ms thick at 25 Hz. How wrong is the pick and in which direction?', '["Four milliseconds late, since the peak follows the base reflection on a thin bed.", "Two milliseconds early, one sample, which is within normal picking precision.", "Four milliseconds early, because the composite is centred on the bed midpoint.", "Not wrong at all, since the peak sits on the top interface at every thickness."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The midpoint is 2 ms below the top and the peak sits about 6 ms above the midpoint on the 2 ms grid, so the pick lands 4 ms early. At 2 ms thickness the error reaches 6 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'Why is a systematic early pick on thin beds more damaging to a map than random picking noise of the same size?', '["Because it is larger than the sample interval and therefore cannot be corrected.", "Because it correlates with thickness and bends the mapped surface.", "Because it changes sign across the structure, which makes the surface rough.", "Because it also biases the amplitude, so two readings are lost together."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The thinnest areas pick earliest, so a bed thinning toward the flanks lifts them and grows the closure. The error does not change sign and nothing about it is visible on the section.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'The 40 Hz tuning thickness is 10 ms and the 25 Hz one is 16 ms. What does the constancy of their product tell you?', '["That the tuning thickness is inversely proportional to frequency, as the wavelet shows.", "That the two frequencies were chosen so that their products would agree.", "That the sample rate of 2 ms divides both thicknesses evenly.", "That the amplitude at tuning must therefore also be inversely proportional to frequency."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With u equal to pi f t, frequency and thickness never appear separately, so one curve serves every frequency. The amplitude at the ideal peak has no frequency in it at all.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Predict the amplitude the 40 Hz model gives at 20 ms of thickness, using the 25 Hz curve alone.', '["0.10669526, the 25 Hz value at 20 ms, since amplitude depends on thickness.", "0.08168091, the 25 Hz value at 32 ms, since 40 times 20 and 25 times 32 are both 800.", "0.11559476, the tuning amplitude, since 20 ms is twice the 40 Hz tuning thickness.", "It cannot be predicted, since each frequency has an independent curve."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Equal products of frequency and thickness give equal amplitudes. The pairing 48 ms at 25 Hz with 30 ms at 40 Hz works the same way, both giving 0.08000147.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'At 15 Hz the model reports a tuning amplitude of 0.1157008, higher than the 0.1155948 reported at 25 and 40 Hz. What is the correct reading of that?', '["A lower frequency genuinely produces a brighter tuned bed, which is why low frequency data shows stronger bright spots.", "The 15 Hz run is in error, since the tuning amplitude cannot depend on frequency.", "The ideal peak is frequency independent, and 15 Hz simply lands nearer it on this grid.", "The 15 Hz wavelet has a deeper side lobe, which raises the whole curve."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ideal is the coefficient times one plus twice the exponential of minus three halves, with no frequency in it. Fifteen hertz tunes at a product of 390 against the ideal 389.85, while the others land on 400.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'A survey is reprocessed from 25 Hz to 40 Hz over a field of beds of many thicknesses. What happens to the amplitude map?', '["Every amplitude rises, since higher frequency data is brighter.", "Every amplitude falls toward the isolated level as interference is reduced.", "The bright ring moves from around 16 ms of bed to around 10 ms of bed.", "Nothing, since amplitudes are ratios and are frequency independent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Whichever bandwidth is delivered, beds near its tuning thickness map brightest, so changing the bandwidth changes which beds those are. An interpreter who has not noticed reads the change as a change in the reservoir.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'What does bandwidth buy, and what does it not buy?', '["It buys a lower tuning thickness and a lower apparent thickness floor.", "It buys a brighter tuned bed and does not change the tuning thickness.", "It buys both a brighter tuned bed and a lower tuning thickness.", "It buys neither, since resolution is set by the reflection coefficients."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every quantity that is a time moves with frequency and every amplitude ratio does not. The brightening factor of 1.4449 and the isolated level are unchanged between the two capstone frequencies.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'A 25 Hz section is picked over a sand and the peak to trough separation reads 13 ms almost everywhere, with a few readings of 15 to 16 ms near a well. What should be concluded?', '["The sand is uniformly about 13 ms thick, which the consistency of the readings supports.", "The readings are at the 13.36 ms floor and carry no thickness information except near the well.", "The picking is unreliable and should be repeated on a reprocessed volume.", "The sand is above tuning everywhere, since 13 ms is close to the 16 ms tuning thickness."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A uniform apparent thickness over a wide area is a warning sign rather than a comfort, because the floor produces exactly that. Only the readings above the floor are measurements.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'State the general form of the Kallweit and Wood result and the specific value it takes for a Ricker wavelet.', '["Tuning occurs at a quarter of a wavelength, which for a Ricker is 0.19492 of a wavelength.", "Tuning occurs at the wavelet''s peak to trough time, at root six over two pi f.", "Tuning occurs at one over 2.6 f, which for a Ricker is root six over two pi f.", "Tuning occurs where the reflection coefficients are largest, which for a Ricker is at 16 ms."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The general result assumes only that the wavelet has a peak and a trough, and the Ricker evaluation follows from differentiating it. The rule of thumb of one over 2.6 f is an approximation to the specific value, not the general form.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Why does the tuning thickness of an opposite pair equal the wavelet''s peak to trough time whatever the wavelet is?', '["Because the composite is largest where the wavelet at the lag is most negative.", "Because the peak of the top reflection and the trough of the base reflection must line up.", "Because the two reflections are equal in magnitude, so their extrema meet halfway.", "Because the wavelet is zero phase, so its trough is symmetric about its peak."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The lag at which a wavelet is most negative is by definition its peak to trough time. The coefficients sit outside the bracket, which is why they scale the answer without moving it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Why does angle dependence interfere with the wedge result on a thin bed?', '["Because the effective wavelet stretches with offset, which moves the tuning thickness.", "Because the search window has to be widened at angle to keep the peak inside it.", "Because reflection coefficients become complex past the critical angle and cannot be convolved.", "Because the top and base of an encased bed stop being exactly opposite once the angle is not zero."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 30 degrees the brine sand''s top reads minus 0.002583 while its base reads plus 0.009937, so the pair the wedge assumes no longer holds and a measured gradient carries a tuning contribution. The full treatment of that interaction belongs to the Rock Physics ladder.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'At 25 Hz the model overshoots theory by 2.60 percent and at 40 Hz by 2.60 percent as well. Why are the fractions equal?', '["Because both frequencies use the same 2 ms sample rate.", "Because the two frequencies are in the ratio 1.6, which cancels in the fraction.", "Because the overshoot is always one fifth of the sample interval.", "Because both runs land on a product of 400 against an ideal product of 389.8484."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Four hundred over 389.8484 is 1.0260 whatever the frequency. Thirty hertz shares the same sample rate and overshoots by 7.7 percent, so the sample rate alone does not produce the equality.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'A 2.6 percent error in the tuning thickness costs only 0.09 percent in the tuning amplitude. What general property does that illustrate?', '["That amplitude is stored with more precision than thickness in the model.", "That the tolerance on the amplitude field is wider than the tolerance on the thickness field.", "That the tuning curve is flat over several samples around its apex.", "That near a maximum the slope is zero, so displacement costs little."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the reason the amplitude fields can carry a generous tolerance and the thickness fields cannot. The flatness of the apex is a description of the same fact rather than an explanation of it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'An interpreter quotes a resolution limit of 20 m at 25 Hz for rock at 2000 m/s. Which convention have they used and what does the model say?', '["The quarter wavelength rule, against a Ricker value of 15.6 m.", "The Kallweit and Wood rule; the model agrees at 20 m.", "The Widess limit; the model gives 10 m, so the claim is conservative.", "The one over 2.6 f rule; the model gives 15.4 m, so the claim is 30 percent optimistic."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A quarter of the 80 m wavelength is 20 m, while root six over four pi is 0.19492 of a wavelength, or 15.6 m. The rule of thumb value of 15.4 m is close to the exact one, not to 20 m.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Which statement about detection and resolution is supported by the model?', '["Both limits are set by bandwidth, so both improve together when the frequency rises.", "Detection and resolution are the same quantity expressed in different conventions.", "Detection stops at the tuning thickness, below which a bed produces no measurable event.", "Resolution is near 16 ms at 25 Hz while detection continues well below it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A 2 ms bed at 25 Hz still gives 0.0243, which is 30 percent of the isolated level. Thin beds go unnoticed because their amplitude falls into the noise, not because the wavelet forbids them.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'What makes agreement between the modelled and the theoretical tuning thickness evidence rather than a tautology?', '["That the theoretical value is more accurate and therefore corrects the model.", "That both are computed from the same wavelet definition and must therefore agree.", "That the residual is smaller than one sample interval.", "That the two routes share nothing but the wavelet definition."]'::jsonb, 3, 'One route convolves and takes an argmax while the other differentiates a closed form. The size of the residual is a separate question from whether the comparison is informative.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'Which error would the check against theory NOT detect?', '["A base coefficient entered with the wrong sign, which moves the argmax to zero thickness.", "A frequency entered as radians per second rather than hertz.", "A search window too narrow to reach the peak on the thinnest beds.", "A reflection pair of plus and minus 0.20 rather than 0.08."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The theoretical value does not involve the coefficients, so it agrees just as well with a wrong pair; the thick end check catches that. A frequency unit error moves both routes together, which is a reminder that a check catches process errors rather than input errors.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Put the wedge workflow steps in the order that protects against the most serious error first.', '["Frequency, then coefficients, then build, then check, then classify the target.", "Build, then check, then coefficients, then frequency, then report.", "Amplitude map first, then build a wedge that reproduces it, then report.", "Coefficients from the logs, then frequency from the data, then build, then check, then classify."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sign of the pair decides whether the tier''s results apply at all, so it comes first. Building a wedge to reproduce a map already in hand turns the model into a rationalisation.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'Why is choosing the model frequency so that the wedge reproduces a known well thickness a mistake?', '["Because the frequency at the target is usually higher than the value that would fit.", "Because the well thickness is measured in metres and the model works in two way time.", "Because the tuning thickness does not depend on frequency, so the fit would be meaningless.", "Because the frequency is a measurement, and fitting it removes the independence that made the model worth building."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A wedge tuned to reproduce one well agrees with that well and with nothing else. The tuning thickness does depend on frequency, which is precisely why fitting it is tempting.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'A wedge reports zero at zero thickness, 0.08 at the thick end and a tuning thickness of 24 ms at 25 Hz. What is the most likely cause?', '["Nothing is wrong, since 24 ms is within one sample of the theoretical 15.59 ms.", "The reflection pair is unequal, which has moved the peak away from 16 ms.", "The sample rate is 4 ms rather than 2 ms, so the grid cannot land on 16.", "A process error such as a narrow search window or a wrong frequency."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The first two checks pass, so the scale and the pair are right, and an unequal opposite pair does not move the tuning thickness. Twenty four is far too distant from 15.59 for any grid to explain.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'A gas substitution lowers a sand''s compressional velocity from 3000 to 2542 m/s and raises its shear velocity from 1700 to 1788 m/s. Why does the shear velocity rise?', '["Because the shear modulus is unchanged while the bulk density falls.", "Because the gas is more compressible, which raises the shear modulus of the frame.", "Because the shear wave travels through the frame only, which is stiffened by the loss of pore pressure.", "Because the substitution recalculates both velocities from the new bulk modulus."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fluid supports no shear, so the shear modulus is Gassmann invariant, and the density falls by the porosity times the fluid density difference. This is the classic signature that separates fluid effects from lithology effects.'),
  ('seismolord', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'After the substitution the sand''s impedance falls from 6,600,000 to 5,055,462 beneath a shale of 5,875,000. What is the consequence for the wedge?', '["The top coefficient flips to minus 0.074977 and the curve scales by 1.29013.", "The tuning thickness falls, since the softer sand has a lower velocity and therefore a shorter wavelength.", "The tuning thickness rises to 20 ms, since the pair is now stronger.", "The wedge cannot be run, since a negative top coefficient breaks the equal and opposite assumption."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tuning amplitude ratio equals the coefficient magnitude ratio to the precision the model stores, and the peak stays at 16 ms because that belongs to the wavelet. A negative pair is still equal and opposite, just the other way round.');
